Data transfer
-------------
For easy transfer of calculation values to the calculator (Psion) or to other programs the global
variables M0..M9 can be used. Simply enter the name of the according global variable ('M0'..'M9') as
a value or press Psion-Enter to automatically enter 'M0' into the selected line. The global variables
M0..M9 can also be used in formulas (e.g. 'y=3*x+M3')!

To store a value in 'M0' press Psion-Shift-Enter. To store all values in lines 1..8 into M1..M8 press
additionally 'Cntrl'.

Press Psion-Help in any dialog to see a list of M0..M8, which can also be changed.

Functions
---------
Beside all standard functions (sin(),..), you can use also the symbol "!" for faculty (n! =
1*2*3*..*n).


Number format
-------------
In all cases you are allowed to enter your values as expressions (e.g. 2*pi/2). The syntax is the
same as used in the system (e.g. calculator). The number format in the examples is with a decimal
point. If you want to use something else, you have to change this from the system.


D A T A B A S E
---------------
The informations for conversion, for the tables and for the equations are contained in the records of
the database. In general the fields have the following meaning:

Group:  <Name of group>     {optional}
Name:   <Name of record>    {must be unique}
        <alias>             {alias names,optional}
        ...
Info:   <Text>              {optional}
        ...                 {more lines (Shift-Enter) are possible}
1       <expr>              {conversion, table, equation or entry}
2       <expr>              {conversion, table, equation or entry}
...
8       <expr>              {conversion, table, equation or entry}

The name of the group is only taken for partitioning (building up an equivalent list). It has no
meaning for the conversion itself.

The name of the record may consist of several (alias) names, which are separated by Shift-Enter on
different lines. These must be unique and will be used alternatively. No numeric (0..9) and special
characters (<blank>*/+-^%.,;()¢[]{}=:) are allowed here.

Info is a general text field to give additional informations about the record.

The expression in field 1 of each record determines how the record will be handled:
1) it is empty: This is a standard unit (SI) record.
2) it contains an expression: This is a conversion record.
3) it contains one or more "=": This is an equation record.
4) it contains one or more TABs: This is a table record.
5) it has a "'" as first character: This is a entry record.

The other fields 2..8 can be empty or are used appropriate.

The program is very dependent on the quality and consisteny of your data files. The files delivered
in the package have to be considered as starting examples and will be (hopefully) extended by you. (I
have to apologize for the sometimes maybe incorrect terms or names in english, but as this is not my
mothertongue language, I collected everything in german and had to translate it).

Hopefully each user will create his own datafiles according to his needs or profession. This is the
main advantage of a open (text based) interface, compared to other programs available.

I can imagine, that many people build up information systems, which would be useful for other peoples
too, and I would like to encourage everyone using my scheme to put his work into public.

All different records are described in more detail in the following.

1) STANDARD UNIT (SI) RECORD (see UNITS.DBF)
----------------------------
These records contain no conversion factors and therefore will be used only to determine the end of
conversion.

Of course, each database should contain all the basic SI units like: kg, m, sec, A, cd, ¡K. But you
could extend it with US$, rad (angel), mol, ...

NOTE: Units, which are more common, should be placed more at the beginning of the database. This is
especially true for the standard units (SI), because the database will be searched for each new unit
again from the beginning. Therefore this determines the speed of the conversion.

2) CONVERSION RECORD (see UNITS.DBF)
--------------------
The conversion expression is either a simple factor (e.g. "12 inch") or a factor with a name in front
(e.g. "Imperial: 5 floz").

In this way it is possible to have alternative conversion factors for the same unit, like in
different measuring systems (US, Imperial, Troy, ..). You choose the appropriate domain by extending
a symbol (unit) with this name (e.g. "inch.imperial" or "inch.us".

The structure of the database is essential for the performing of the program. Here some comments:

Each unit must be solvable (in general via other units) to the standard units, for example: knots ->
seamile/h; seameile -> meter ; h -> min -> seconds.
If a unit can not be found, a warning will be generated.
Avoid circular dependencies like foot = 12 inch; inch = 1/12 foot.

The search algorithm determines how units are found (see above). Avoid ambiguities with the scaling
factors. Note, that if a unit is named "nm", it is not possible anymore to find the unit nanometer =
"nm".

Of course the speed of the conversion will be much higher if dependent units will be defined directly
equivalent to the SI units.
e.g. mile = 1609 m; fathom = 1.82 m; yard = 0.914 m ; foot = 0.3 m; ...
But I think (and this was one reason to write the program) it is much more elegant (and more precise)
to define the conversions according the underlying system and let the program itself evaluate the
conversion factor by searching through all units. e.g. mile = 8 fathom; fathom = 2 yard; yard = 3
foot; foot=12 inch ; inch = 2.54 cm;

To see if your database performs consistently, use the function 'Report' to generate a list of all
your records with evaluation to standard units. Unresolvable units will be flagged with an error
remark.

3) EQUATION RECORD (see EQUATION.DBF)
------------------
An example could be the formula from mathematics to evaluate the volume of a cone. If you look in a
formulary it is:

volume = radius**2 * pi * height/3.

'radius' and 'height' are the so called INDEPENDENT variables, which are entered.
'volume' is the so called DEPENDENT variable, which you want.
The equation can be always transformed e.g. to calculate the 'height' from given 'volume' and
'radius'!

The program analyses the equation system and determines all dependent variables. If there are more
possibilities, the program asks you for the variable to be calculated!

Each record contains several (related or simple) equations.

The syntax for the equations is: ([]=optional; \=or):

	symbol [label] ['='\'=='formula] [';'[initial_value]]

'symbol' is the name of dependent variable of the equation in this line (the function result).
'label' is any text, explaining the variable.
'=' starts the formula, the formula will be displayed.
'==' starts the formula, the formula will not be displayed.
'formula' is the actual functional expression. 
';initial_value' is a starting value (default 1).

Examples:

g gravity/m/s2=v/s;9.81	{the initial value for g will be 9.81}
g gravity/m/s2			{the value for g can only be set; initial default 1}
gravity/m/s2			{the label "gravity" is the symbol, characters after '/' are ignored}

Formulas F(a,b,c,d,..)
--------
Each line holds the appropriate transformation (equation) of the formula to be taken for evaluation
of the independent variable from all other (dependent) variables.

a=A(b,c,d,..)
b=B(a,c,d,..)
...

The data record for the cone example above could look like that:

Group:	Mathematics
Name:	Cone
Info:	Threedimensional object
1:		V volume=r**2*pi*h/3
2:		r radius=sqr(V/(pi*h/3))
3:		h height=V/(r**2*pi)*3

You are allowed to enter values on each line. The program always asks which line to calculate (from
the others!)

The program always analyzes the complete formula system to determine the dependent and independent
variables and to decide which line can be set and which choices for calculation or plotting are
possible! If you are not able to recalculate a formula to the approriate transformation and you want
this variable to be set, you should at least denote this with a list of the independent variables
(embraced with {}) in this line. The value can not be calculated (obviously) but can then be set,
eg.:

k=y-x**5+x**3+..
y=x**5+x**3+...+k
x={k,y}


Equations
---------
Equations which are not related to the same formula, but use variables from other equations will be
simply evaluated in the order top to down.

See the following extended example:

Group:	Mathematics
Name:	Cone
Info:	V = r**2 * pi *h/3
1:		V volume:r**2*pi*h/3
2:		r radius:sqr(V/(pi*h/3))
3:		h height:V/(r**2*pi)*3
4:		-------------------------------
5:		M coat_area=r*pi*sqr(r**2+h**2)
6:		G ground_aera=r**2*pi
7:		O surface=G+M

The variables in the last 3 lines (5..7) will be additionally calculated (from the variables in the
previous lines), but can not be set.


Plotting
--------
In entering a value range with '..' (e.g. '-10..10') for a variable you select the plot mode. This
(with ENTER) selected variable will always be considered as the independent variable on the
horizontal axis.
The program analyses the equation system and asks you for the dependent equation to be plotted on the
vertical axis!
If you have also defined a value range for the dependent variable, this range will be used as value
range for the function. Otherwise the range will be evaluated (autoranging) and entered as a function
value!
For multiparametric plots you can change now a parameter and plot the same function as an overlay
into the same coordinate system. Simply select the same variables for the horizontal and vertical
axis.


4) TABLE RECORD (see TABLES.DBF)
---------------
Tables describe how variables correlate to each other. A simple example is a table of currencies. A
more complex example can be seen in the different temperature scales.

There a 3 different possibilities to define a table:

1) SIMPLE LINEAR FUNCTION (y=k*x)
Each value ist only multiplied by a factor. In this case the formula is represented by a (example)
constant.

The formulas are simply a table of values or expressions([]=optional):

label 'tab'[..'tab'] expression

'label' is any text for notifing the value.
'tab' is the delimiter.
'expression' is simply a number or an algebraic expression.

Examples:

Group:  Currency
Name:   Europe
Info:
1:      Germany/DM          1
2:      France/ffr          3.25
3:      Switzerland/sfr     1/1.22
4:      England/Pfund       1/2.94

Group:  Length
Name:   Imperial
1:      mile                1
2:      furlong             8
3:      chain               8*10
4:      rod                 8*10*4
5:      yard                8*10*22
6:      foot                8*10*22*3
7:      inch                8*10*22*3*12

2) LINEAR FUNCTION (y=a+b*x)
Each value is given through a value range, which corresponds to the ranges in the other lines. In
this case this range is defined through two (example) constants.

The formulas are a table of value ranges or expressions([]=optional):

label 'tab'[..'tab'] expression1 ".." expression2

'label' is any text for notifing the value.
'tab' is the delimiter.
'expression' is simply a number or an algebraic expression.

Example:

Group:  Sizes
Name:   Shoes_Men
Info:
1:      England             6.5..11
2:      USA                 7..(11+1/2)	{or simply 11.5!}
3:      Europe              39..46

The program interpolates or extrapolates accordingly (the range can also be reversed)!

3) ANY FUNCTION
Any functional relation (nonlinear) can be used.

Each equation in a line always uses a dependent (output) value of another line as the independent
(input) value. Therefore there exists a circular dependency of all equations (not necessarily top to
down!), like

a=A(b)
b=B(c)
c=C(a)

Example:

Group:  Physics
Name:   Temperatures
Info: 
1:      Kelvin=Rankine*5/9;273.15
2:      Celsius=Kelvin-273.15
3:      Fahrenheit=9/5*Celsius+32
4:      Reaumur=100/80*(Fahrenheit-32)*5/9
5:      Rankine=9/5*(80/100*Reaumur+273.15)

You see: Kelvin=f(Rankine); Celsius=f(Kelvin); Fahrenheit=f(Celsius); ...

NOTE: If you change to a table in the same group, the program keeps the previous factor (linear
functions) or the first value (nonlinear functions) and uses it in the new table. This is very handy
if you have to change between different tables (like currencies). BUT: You have to relate the tables
to the same value (e.g. DM).

5) ENTRY RECORD
---------------
These records are only to hold your favourite entry strings and to allow you to select it with the
name of the record plus the number of the string.
eg. "Func4" selects the string in field 4 of record "Func".


I hope this short explanation gives you an idea how to write your own personal data records.

UNITS, TABLES and EQUATION records are separated into the three example files only for clarity. You
certainly can combine (merge) all record types into one general database file (recommended).
